/// A filesystem on a removable drive, mounted or not, keyed by `device`.
record Volume key device {
  /// Its block device: `/dev/sdb1` (an unlocked encrypted one's
  /// `/dev/mapper/…`), as `Filesystem.device` names it once mounted.
  device: text
  /// Its label (`STICK`), or null when it has none:
  /// `v.label ?? bytes(v.size)`.
  label: text?
  /// Its size in bytes.
  size: float
  /// It is mounted.
  mounted: bool
  /// Where it is mounted (`/run/media/you/STICK`), or null. Once mounted
  /// it is in `disk.all` under this `path`, with its usage.
  path: text?
  /// Mounts it through UDisks2, where UDisks2 chooses
  /// (`/run/media/$USER/<label>`). A removable drive's filesystem mounts
  /// without a password on most systems; where polkit asks for one, the
  /// session's polkit agent asks (Strand's own while a config reads
  /// `polkit`). Asked again while one runs (a double click), it is
  /// dropped. A failure is `drives.failed`.
  action mount()
  /// Unmounts it. A program holding a file open on it makes it fail
  /// (`target is busy`), as `drives.failed`. Asked again while one runs,
  /// it is dropped.
  action unmount()
}

/// A removable drive with its medium in (a USB stick or disk, a memory
/// card, a disc), keyed by `device`.
record Drive key device {
  /// Its block device: `/dev/sdb`, `/dev/sr0`.
  device: text
  /// Its vendor and model (`Kingston DataTraveler 3.0`), or its device
  /// when it names neither.
  name: text
  /// Its size in bytes (its medium's, for a card or a disc).
  size: float
  /// An icon name for its kind: `media-optical-symbolic` for a disc
  /// drive, `media-flash-symbolic` for a stick or a card,
  /// `drive-removable-media-symbolic` otherwise.
  icon: text
  /// `eject()` can make it safe to remove: UDisks2 can eject its medium
  /// or power it off.
  can_eject: bool
  /// Its filesystems, keyed by `device`, in partition order. Filesystems
  /// UDisks2 is told to hide, and encrypted ones still locked, are left out.
  volumes: [Volume]
  /// Makes it safe to remove. It unmounts each of its mounted filesystems
  /// (and locks an encrypted one unlocked), then takes one last step:
  /// it ejects the medium where the medium comes out (a disc, a card),
  /// leaving the drive on for the next one, and otherwise powers the
  /// drive off where it can be (a USB stick or disk). The drive then
  /// leaves `drives.all`. The first step refused (a filesystem in use)
  /// stops it, as `drives.failed`. With `can_eject` false it fails at
  /// once and unmounts nothing. Asked again while one runs, it is
  /// dropped; once started it finishes, even if the shell stops reading
  /// `drives` meanwhile.
  action eject()
}

/// Removable drives and their filesystems, from UDisks2, followed live
/// as they are plugged in, mounted, unmounted and ejected. Internal disks,
/// loop devices and drives with no medium in (an empty card reader or
/// disc drive) are not listed. `disk` has the mounted filesystems' usage.
service drives {
  /// Every removable drive with its medium in, keyed by `device`: those
  /// there when UDisks2 started first, then in the order plugged in.
  all: [Drive]
  /// UDisks2 answers. False without it (or without a system bus), so a
  /// menu can hide what cannot work.
  available: bool
  /// Mounting, unmounting or ejecting failed (UDisks2 or polkit refused
  /// it, a filesystem was busy, UDisks2 or the system bus was missing):
  /// `on drives.failed(device, error) { … }`. `device` is the volume's for
  /// `mount()` and `unmount()` and the drive's for `eject()`; `error` is
  /// what UDisks2 says.
  event failed(device: text, error: text)
}
